# Carrier Wound #25: The Normalize-Recovery Copy Aborted The Lane Blind

Wound #24 (the cap stopped counting corpses) shipped and a fresh dream run
`run-live-20260614T140159124Z-70714011` was **admitted** — proving the cap-death
fix works live. Then it blocked, terminal, ~40 minutes later. The admin feed told
the story node-by-node: **three drive generations** (14:04 → 14:17 → 14:32), each
re-planning, then terminal `adapter_unavailable` at 14:44. The planner lane died
the same deterministic way three times and the run was re-driven against it
anyway. The blocker text named the step — `normalize-output (exit 1, pi 0)` — and
then showed, as its only evidence, the benign clone log: *"You appear to have
cloned an empty repository."* pi exited **0**. So what halted at normalize-output
with a non-zero exit, and why was its only cause a line about an empty clone?

## Mechanism — a recovery path that could itself fail, under `set -eu`, blind

The agent lane runs under `set -eu` (`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lane-command.ts`).
After pi exits, the JSON lanes normalize pi's raw output into the blueprint/verdict
envelope. Wound #20 made a failed normalize an **honest** signal —
`output_normalized=0`, `outputNormalization.reason` recorded — that must NOT
clobber `pi_status`, so the receipt commits `status:"failed"` instead of forging
an adapter outage. The recovery branch copied pi's raw output to `LANE_OUTPUT_PATH`
so that committed receipt still had a body:

```bash
else
  output_normalized=0
  cp "$raw_output_path" "$LANE_OUTPUT_PATH"   # <-- BARE cp, under set -eu
fi
```

That `cp` is **itself fallible** — an oversized raw output against a full
lite-instance disk (the wound #23-B failure shape), or a missing/short-read
source. And a bare `cp` that exits non-zero under `set -eu` **aborts the entire
lane at exit 1**, at step normalize-output, *before the receipt is ever built*.
Worse, the `cp` error went to the **script's own stderr** — an uncaptured channel.
`$stderr_path` held only pi's (empty) stderr; `$git_log_path` held the benign
empty-repo clone line. So `selectLaneAbortDiagnostic` (piRan ⇒ `stderrTail ||
gitLogTail`) had nothing in stderrTail and fell back to the clone log. The marker
was **blind**: it named the step and showed an unrelated, reassuring line.

Then the planner adapter compounded it. `createCloudflarePiPlannerLaneAdapter`
got no receipt at all (the lane aborted), so `runLane` threw the abort diagnostic,
which `proposeDynamicWorkflowBlueprint` (`workflow-app.ts`) catches and forges into
**`adapter_unavailable`** — a code that means *transport/infra is down*. It wasn't.
pi ran fine and produced output the planner couldn't use. `adapter_unavailable`
read as transient, so the run was re-driven three generations against a failure
that was 100% deterministic.

This is wound #23-B's promise **left incomplete**: #23-B made oversized output a
fast honest failure *the normalizer* reports, but the recovery copy could still
abort the lane before that honest receipt was committed — so the receipt #23-B
intended never got written, and the operator saw a blind `adapter_unavailable`
instead of `reason: oversized_output`. Like every wound this week, it was only
visible because the run was watched node-by-node, not stared at —
*"feels like we should be able to observe the run in progress and not guess if it's
functioning instead of waiting and staring at it like fucking dummies."*

> A Philosophy of Software Design (John Ousterhout), Ch. 10, "Defining Errors Out
> of Existence":
>
> > The best way to [reduce where exceptions must be handled] is by redefining
> > semantics to eliminate error conditions. For exceptions that can't be defined
> > away, you should look for opportunities to mask them at a low level, so their
> > impact is limited...
>
> (citing Ding Yuan et al., *"Simple Testing Can Prevent Most Critical Failures,"*
> OSDI 2014 — the failures that take systems down live in the least-tested
> error-handling path, and a simple test would have caught them.)

The recovery copy is exactly such an error-handling path: rarely exercised,
catastrophic when it fires, and able to fail in a way that masked its own cause.

## Fix — mask the recovery's failure at a low level; never abort, never go blind

Two surgical changes, no new abstraction.

### A — the recovery copy can no longer abort or hide (carrier core)

A single `persist_unnormalized_output` helper replaces both bare `cp` sites. It
**never aborts**, **tees any failure into `$stderr_path`** so the marker carries
the real cause, and **guarantees `LANE_OUTPUT_PATH` exists** (a placeholder if the
copy fails) so the downstream hash/add/commit steps run and a failed normalize
**always yields a committed `status:"failed"` receipt**:

```bash
persist_unnormalized_output() {
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$LANE_OUTPUT_PATH")" 2>>"$stderr_path" || true
  if ! cp "$raw_output_path" "$LANE_OUTPUT_PATH" 2>>"$stderr_path"; then
    printf '[agent-lane recovery] could not persist raw pi output to LANE_OUTPUT_PATH; see stderr tail for the copy failure\n' \
      > "$LANE_OUTPUT_PATH" 2>>"$stderr_path" || true
  fi
}
```

The error condition is *defined out of existence* for the lane's control flow: the
copy's failure is masked at the lowest level, its impact limited to a placeholder
body and a captured stderr line — it can no longer take down the whole lane.

### B — the planner names the real cause instead of a blind generic throw

The verifier adapter already read `receipt.outputNormalization` and reported
*"agent produced no parseable verdict (reason: ...)"*. The planner adapter was its
un-classified twin — a generic *"did not return a completed real-agent receipt."*
Now (post-A) the planner lane commits a `status:"failed"` receipt with the real
`outputNormalization.reason`, and the adapter surfaces it:

```ts
if (normalization?.normalized === false) {
  cause = `planner produced no parseable blueprint (reason: ${
    normalization.reason ?? "unknown"
  }${stopReasonSuffix})`;
}
```

So the blocker now reads *"...planner produced no parseable blueprint (reason:
no_parseable_output)"* — the observability the governing demand asked for. The
operator stops guessing; the next re-drive decision is made on a named cause.

## Chaos Receipt (would have caught it)

`tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts` — **31 passing** (1
new). The new test is a hostile double in the file's established style: it slices
the **real** normalize-output recovery block out of `buildPiAgentLaneCommand()` and
runs it verbatim under production's `set -eu`, with the JSON normalizer forced to
fail (fake `node` exits 1) **and the raw-output source missing** so the recovery
copy cannot succeed — the exact production conditions. It asserts the block
**(1) does not abort** (reaches a post-recovery sentinel), **(2) leaves an output
file** (the placeholder, so downstream steps never abort), and **(3) captures the
copy error into `$stderr_path`** (the marker is not blind). Against the pre-fix
bare `cp` all three flip: it aborts before the sentinel, leaves no file, and sends
the error to the harness's own stderr. A polite test would have copied a file that
exists; this one constructs the failing-copy state the production lane actually got
into.

## Verification

```bash
pnpm vitest run tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts  # 31 pass (1 new)
pnpm vitest run                                                            # 412 pass, 55 files
pnpm typecheck   # clean (tsgo)
pnpm check       # oxfmt + oxlint + type-aware, clean
```

## Known follow-ups (not in this fix)

- **Deterministic re-drive (3 generations / 40 min).** With B, the blocker is now
  honest and fast, but the driver still re-drove planning three times before going
  terminal. A planner that fails deterministically should fail fast — assess once
  a clean named-cause block is observed live; may fold into Defect 2's pause/re-arm
  seam rather than a separate retry cap.
- **`adapter_unavailable` overload (Defect 2 family).** A planner-no-plan is still
  routed through `proposeDynamicWorkflowBlueprint`'s catch into
  `adapter_unavailable`. B fixes the *message*; the *code* still conflates a
  no-plan with a transport outage. Reclassify alongside the backpressure pause work.
- **The deepest question this fix now lets us READ:** *why* does pi produce no
  parseable blueprint (no_parseable_output vs oversized_output vs agent_error)?
  Before this fix that was a blind wall; after it, the committed receipt's
  `outputNormalization.reason` names it on the next live run. Diagnose from the
  named reason — do not guess.

## Burn-In Gate

Per the Phase 2 D3 acceptance bar, the 10-run count stays at **zero**. This fix
deploys, then ONE dream-shape run is watched node-by-node — either to `captured`
(pi produced a real blueprint, the carrier carried it) or to an **honest, named**
block (`reason: ...`) that tells us the next carrier move without staring. Only a
`captured` on fixed, deployed code fires the operator Discord ping with the wzrrd
HITL URL, resets D3, and starts the serial burn-in. Sibling of
[[carrier-wound-cap-death-spiral-2026-06-14]]: #24 made admission stop counting the
dead; #25 makes the recovery path stop killing the lane in silence. *Any failure is
a carrier failure: fix the class in the carrier, with a chaos test that would have
caught it, reset the count.*
